### Verification notes (honest gaps)

- **Coverage:** Federal primary (4) + secondary gateways (2); **51** state/DC rows; **5** territories with a verifiable official public site.
- **New York (OCFS):** `https://ocfs.ny.gov/` is the widely cited official agency site (ACF / public directories). Live page fetch from this environment was intermittent (connection errors / 500); treat as the correct official URL and re-check in-browser if needed.
- **Ohio (DCY):** `https://childrenandyouth.ohio.gov/` content verified (Department of Children and Youth). Some automated clients receive HTTP 404; confirm in a normal browser if a link appears broken.
- **American Samoa:** Public site responded on **HTTP** (`http://www.dhss.as/`); HTTPS did not respond from this environment. Page content confirms children/family welfare including protective services.
- **CNMI (`dys.gov.mp`):** Page content previously retrieved successfully; some clients receive HTTP 406—retry in a browser if needed.
- **Bot-protected portals:** Arizona (`dcs.az.gov`), Arkansas, Colorado, Louisiana, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Rhode Island, and Utah official URLs are listed from government sources / successful content checks; some return HTTP 403 to automated fetchers. Prefer opening in a browser rather than inventing alternate URLs.
- **Pennsylvania:** County-administered child welfare under state DHS oversight; listed URL is the state DHS public homepage (no separate stable statewide CPS-only homepage verified as distinct).
- **Minnesota:** Child welfare programs moved under the Department of Children, Youth, and Families (`dcyf.mn.gov`); county/Tribal delivery still applies for many CPS functions.
- Do **not** invent replacement URLs. If a link fails, use CWIG State/Territory Resources or ACF’s state human-services directory to locate the current official page.

## Done criteria

- Inbound email at **barbba.cursor@gmail.com** monitored and handled: questions answered professionally in **ask mode**; directives processed via normal Barb intake under existing rules (escalate unclear scope/priority to Paul).
- Work package(s) handed to Dave Dev with problem, scope, actors, requirements, acceptance criteria, data-sensitivity notes, and open questions filled.
- Matching **user-story pick-up task(s)** posted in Slack in the **project-solution** workspace (story shape, acceptance criteria, links/refs to package detail)—synthetic/redacted data only.
- Test scenarios handed to Tony Tester, mapped to those acceptance criteria / work packages, with preconditions, steps, and expected results clear enough for script development (no executable scripts from Barb).
- Design choices favor efficient case-worker workflow and best value for the child; tradeoffs are stated, not implied.
- Data-protection principles (least privilege, need-to-know, minimization, auditability) are addressed for any sensitive data touched in packages, Slack tasks, scenarios, and **email replies**.
- No invented legal citations, mailbox credentials, or API keys; assumptions and blockers are explicit; public sources cited when relied on in email or packages.
- Paul PM can see package, Slack pick-up, and scenario status and any decisions needed; Dave can claim a project-solution story and start without guessing core behavior; Tony can script without inventing expected results.
